WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:27.913
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:27.913 --> 00:00:29.880
Welcome to the Homeroom live streams.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.380
Sal here from Khan Academy,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.470
very excited about
today's guest Hasan Minhaj

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.260
and I encourage everyone
watching on Facebook or YouTube.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.012
If you have questions for Has or myself,

00:00:39.012 --> 00:00:41.350
feel free to start put
putting those questions

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:43.550
in the message board
below and we will get to

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:45.180
as many of those as possible.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.440
But before we jump into the conversation,

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.026
I'll give my standard
announcement reminder to everyone

00:00:50.026 --> 00:00:52.710
that Khan Academy is a
not-for-profit organization.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.750
We can only exist through donations,

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.660
philanthropic donations
from folks like yourself.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:58.120
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.320
please think about going
to KhanAcademy.org/donate.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.440
Also want to give a special
shout out to several

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:04.890
organizations that stepped up.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.810
You can imagine, even
before COVID Khan Academy

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:10.420
was running at a deficit
and then COVID hit,

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.850
our traffic became 300% of normal,

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.250
and our deficit grew even larger.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.410
And so a special thanks
to Bank of America,

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:21.022
Google.org, AT&amp;T, Fastly, Novartis,

00:01:21.022 --> 00:01:23.800
Amgen Foundation and others
who've helped us close that gap.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.750
But we still have one, so
any help you can provide

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:28.450
is very much appreciated.

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:30.560
So with that, I'm super excited

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.700
to introduce our guest Hasan Minhaj.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.910
I think, probably doesn't
need much of an introduction

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:39.080
for a lot of folks, but I consider Hasan,

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.230
me and my entire family are huge fans.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:44.580
He's kind of one of the actors, comedians,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.100
entertainers of our time known

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.330
for "The Patriot Act", "Homecoming King",

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.980
he was a correspondent
on "The Daily Show".

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.640
So thanks for so much for joining Hasan.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:55.990
- Thanks for having me Sal.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.700
- So maybe-
- Man, that means a lot

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.332
that I mean a lot to your family.

00:02:00.332 --> 00:02:02.313
That was a very warm welcome.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:05.900
- Well, it means a lot
that it means a lot to you

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:09.523
that it means a lot to my family. (laughs)

00:02:10.644 --> 00:02:14.580
Let's just start off a little bit on,

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.423
I think something that
you've addressed a lot

00:02:16.423 --> 00:02:20.696
in your speeches or your comedy,

00:02:20.696 --> 00:02:23.499
but I think, especially as a South Asian,

00:02:23.499 --> 00:02:25.310
it has a lot of resonance.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:26.680
I'm suspecting that we're going to have

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.890
a lot of South Asians,
especially, tuning in today.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:32.870
Is this what you expected you
were going to do when you were

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:35.130
a teenager or when you were younger?

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.500
Did you think you were
going to be an entertainer?

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.873
- No, not at all, man.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.210
Look, I was just like every
other desi kid kid growing up.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.080
I was pre-med, I failed it pre-med.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.075
Then I tried to do pre-law.

00:02:47.075 --> 00:02:50.370
I took the LSAT, I lied to my parents,

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.030
and then I started doing comedy.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:57.490
So no, I had no idea that I'd be able

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:58.670
to have a career doing this,

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:00.783
and I still don't fully believe it.

00:03:01.787 --> 00:03:04.490
- (laughs) And just to
clarify some terminology,

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:06.221
the word you just used, desi,

00:03:06.221 --> 00:03:09.170
it means, depending on the language

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.300
in the Indian subcontinent,
in Bengali it's deshi,

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:14.070
but it means someone from your country,

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.030
but it generally refers to people

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.434
from the South Asian, Indian continent.

00:03:18.434 --> 00:03:20.710
- People from the South
Asian diaspora, yes.

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:22.403
- Exactly, exactly.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.110
So what was it, given that there weren't

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:29.440
a lot of other folks
from, we're essentially

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:31.170
the same heritage, from our background

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:32.430
going into entertainment.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.117
What was it about you that you said,

00:03:34.117 --> 00:03:35.700
"Hey, maybe this is something I can do."

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.140
Was it a talent you had?

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.157
Did people say, "You're really funny,

00:03:38.157 --> 00:03:39.370
"You should be comedian,"

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:41.220
or just like, that's the life I want?

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.940
- No, so look, I grew up
like a lot of kids where

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:48.890
we didn't have cable television at home,

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:52.430
so I wasn't a pop culture junkie as a kid,

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:54.550
but I was really into speech and debate.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.690
That was one of the things I
was naturally kind of good at.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.840
And I found when I was doing
speech and debate tournaments

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.430
and public speaking
tournaments in high school,

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.980
I just naturally had an
inclination to either

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:08.810
make fun of the prompt or the
person that I was debating.

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:11.400
And every time I did that,
the judges would give me

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.697
10 to 15 points higher
because they're just parents

00:04:13.697 --> 00:04:15.300
who are bored out of their minds.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.830
And so the fact that like some skinny,

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:20.440
desi kid is making fun of
someone in a high school gym

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:21.860
is really funny to them.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:24.130
And I didn't realize until
later when I got to college

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.920
and actually started
watching standup comedy,

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.320
it clicked for me in that
moment my freshman year,

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.507
I was like, "Oh, stand up comedy is just

00:04:31.507 --> 00:04:33.140
"funny speech and debate."

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.830
And so everything else I had
really kind of failed at,

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:38.503
I wasn't that great at it.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:43.398
I used to work at Safeway,
I got fired from Safeway.

00:04:43.398 --> 00:04:47.154
I used to work at Office Max,
I got fired from Office Max.

00:04:47.154 --> 00:04:49.110
I used to work at a tech company,

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.590
I got fired from that tech company.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.323
Just, I was really not
good at anything else.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.640
- Well, you're a step better than me.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.100
Actually in my first job, I
remember I applied for both

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.930
McDonald's and Wendy's and
I didn't even get the job.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:02.010
So at least you got

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:03.043
your foot in the door.
- You didn't get it?

00:05:03.043 --> 00:05:04.750
Did you lie on your application

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:07.890
or were you just like really
straight forward and honest?

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.990
- No, I mean, I was just
looking for some extra money.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:13.760
I don't know why I would
lie on the application,

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:15.430
but yeah, at least you got the job.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:17.470
I am curious, why do
you think you got fired?

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:18.960
And I suspect you might be exaggerating,

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.240
but why did you get fired?

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.710
- So with Safeway, I
was stressed out one day

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:28.130
and I just kind of snapped
at work, that wasn't good.

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:30.526
And then at Office Max, I was
supposed to sell printers,

00:05:30.526 --> 00:05:32.710
and so whenever someone
comes into the store,

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:35.167
you're supposed to say
"Welcome to Office Max,

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:37.420
"how can I help you take it to the max?"

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.930
And there used to be people from my school

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.210
that would come in and this
girl that I had a huge crush on,

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.467
she came into Office Max one
day, and I just didn't say it.

00:05:45.467 --> 00:05:48.580
And my manager was like,
"Hey, you gotta say it."

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:50.457
And I'm like, "I don't want to say it."

00:05:50.457 --> 00:05:52.410
"Well you gotta say it," and he really

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.740
saw that as like mutiny.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:55.927
And then I got one of those, like,

00:05:55.927 --> 00:05:57.677
"Hasan can come to the back, please?

00:05:57.677 --> 00:05:58.830
"Hasan to the back,"

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.757
and I was like, "Oh
shit, I'm gonna have to

00:06:00.757 --> 00:06:02.870
"turn in my name tag."

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:05.665
- Okay, yeah, so are legitimate firings.

00:06:05.665 --> 00:06:07.696
You were insubordinate.

00:06:07.696 --> 00:06:09.173
(laughing)
- I was just an employee

00:06:09.173 --> 00:06:11.760
Even when I was working,
when I had graduated college,

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.920
I had been doing standup for
about four years at that point

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.260
from my freshman year,
I'm in my early twenties,

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:18.270
and I moved to San Francisco.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:20.490
They had a great comedy scene there,

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:21.980
and I worked at a tech company

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.470
and I just wasn't getting my work done.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:27.220
And I remember my manager
telling me you're very capable,

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:30.170
but it seems like you
want to be somewhere else.

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:31.177
And I was like, yeah, I kind of

00:06:31.177 --> 00:06:32.913
do want to be somewhere else.

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:36.777
It was a mutual thing where she was like,

00:06:36.777 --> 00:06:38.780
"This is not the right fit,
you should probably go."

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:41.610
And then, I mean, you might
know this, Sal, very well.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:45.510
In Silicon Valley, someone
walks you out of the building.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:48.327
So like an attorney, like some
sort of like HR attorney guy.

00:06:48.327 --> 00:06:50.320
Had to walk me out of the building,

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:52.970
down the street to the
Coffee Bean in Palo Alto

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:54.850
and then gave me my severance check.

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:56.830
It was really weird, and I was
like, "Why did you do that?"

00:06:56.830 --> 00:06:58.427
And he's like, "Well, sometimes
when we fire employees,

00:06:58.427 --> 00:07:00.827
"people go postal, we have to do this.

00:07:00.827 --> 00:07:02.047
"They'll smash computers,

00:07:02.047 --> 00:07:04.600
"and we had to do this out of safety."

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.520
And I was kinda touched that he thought

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:11.520
155 pound Indian dude would
wreak havoc inside of a...

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:15.540
- Those are the guys you
got to worry about the most.

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:17.643
- Yeah, I actually took
it as a compliment.

00:07:17.643 --> 00:07:19.340
I'm looking really big in these mediums

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:20.890
if you think that I'm a threat.

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:23.610
- Yeah, I've never had the walk.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:25.230
I have definitely been in conversations

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.540
where we thought about
whether we had to do the walk,

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:29.640
but then we decided that
that was not appropriate.

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:32.570
We decided we trusted the
person that, you know.

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:37.570
But on your career, a lot of
times when you look at someone,

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:40.247
like yourself and you're just like,

00:07:40.247 --> 00:07:41.807
"Oh, he realized he was funny.

00:07:41.807 --> 00:07:42.867
"He realized he had a gift.

00:07:42.867 --> 00:07:43.757
"He started doing standup,

00:07:43.757 --> 00:07:45.295
"and then he becomes Hasan Minhaj."

00:07:45.295 --> 00:07:49.700
Did it work that way, or was
it a little bit difficult?

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:51.180
- Now, let me ask you an honest question,

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:52.470
were from the same background.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.797
Out of all your cousins and
out of your entire khandan,

00:07:54.797 --> 00:07:56.470
are you the smartest?

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:57.930
You're definitely the most successful,

00:07:57.930 --> 00:07:59.230
but were you the smartest?

00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:04.947
- No, I would say growing
up, my sister was the one-

00:08:04.947 --> 00:08:06.973
- What's your sister's name?

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:09.060
- Farrah.

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:10.997
- Farrah, so everybody's
like Farrah's so smart,

00:08:10.997 --> 00:08:13.163
Farrah's the best, right?

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:16.900
Did you ever feel like
everything that you do,

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:18.213
you're just diet Farrah?

00:08:20.699 --> 00:08:22.180
Academically, academically.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:24.870
- Yeah, I would say for about
the first 14 years of my life,

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:28.270
I started to getting Farrah
parody when I was like 15,

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:29.460
but people still didn't see it.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:31.220
I knew it, but they didn't see it

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:33.253
'cause my brand had been defined.

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:37.820
Got it, yeah, so for
me, look, I had a lot of

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:40.116
really, really smart,
intelligent cousins that,

00:08:40.116 --> 00:08:42.870
my little sister she's
way smarter than me.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:45.370
She went to an Ivy League
school, all of those things.

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:48.983
And so I quickly realized
as I was in my twenties,

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:53.103
I can try to run this race with them,

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.992
but I will always a
diet version of Ayesha.

00:08:56.992 --> 00:09:00.540
I'm not going to beat her
at standardized testing.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:02.590
I'm not going to beat her
at these certain things.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:04.590
I'm really good at these other things.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:07.970
And society didn't necessarily value them,

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:10.605
our community definitely
didn't value them,

00:09:10.605 --> 00:09:12.590
but I found value in them.

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:16.523
And I felt like I had a
natural inclination for it.

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:18.810
The same way there's certain kids that

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.140
I remember in calculus
BC, they just got it.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:23.840
Like math, they just got
it verbal, they got it.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.350
They could just ingest tons of material,

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:28.960
understand it, and then
regurgitate it back out.

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:30.659
I had that with public
speaking or thinking about

00:09:30.659 --> 00:09:35.310
a quick joke on the fly or
constructing a funny story.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:37.247
And so I just said, "Look, I'm going to be

00:09:37.247 --> 00:09:38.597
"the best version of myself,

00:09:38.597 --> 00:09:41.638
"and I'm gonna just go all in on comedy."

00:09:41.638 --> 00:09:46.560
And really after a while I didn't have

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:48.440
a high enough self esteem, ego,

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:51.410
or just outlook on life where I felt like

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:53.360
anything else was gonna
work out for me in that way,

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:56.200
so I just decided to kind of put

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:58.780
med school and law school
hustle into this comedy thing.

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:01.940
And then I became very lucky,
that's really what it is.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:04.393
It's not due to my just
work, I got very lucky.

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:07.470
- But what I am curious about
when you took that leap,

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:08.450
and as we've talked about already,

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:09.900
it's not the standard leap,

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:11.523
especially from folks from our community.

00:10:11.523 --> 00:10:14.176
I mean, you really are
one of the pioneers here.

00:10:14.176 --> 00:10:17.450
When you started it, did
you have moments of doubt

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:19.487
where you're just like, "Oh,
maybe this was the mistake.

00:10:19.487 --> 00:10:22.610
"Maybe my parents were
right," or whatever else.

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:25.310
- Yeah so there were some
seminal moments for me.

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:28.960
I remember 2004, I was
walking around Davis,

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.780
that's the town that I grew
up in, I went to UC Davis.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:33.510
I know there's probably
people that are watching

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:35.930
that are very disappointed, but, you know,

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:37.750
I got a 1310 on the SAT.

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:39.090
So I was at UC Davis I'm walking-

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:41.470
- It's amazing how many people
are asking about your SATs?

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:44.103
I don't know if this is
a thing, but I'm tryin-

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:47.050
- I got a 1310, and we can
include this in the chat,

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:50.853
I retook it on the show just
as a joke, and I did worse.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.110
- It's interesting, it tells
you a lot about our community,

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:57.860
how many South Asians are
asking about your SAT scores.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:01.300
But anyways, sorry I interrupted.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:02.600
- The fact that I'm willing to admit it

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:04.623
means that I'm a Vanguard and I'm brave.

00:11:05.826 --> 00:11:06.659
- It is.
- They're like,

00:11:06.659 --> 00:11:07.840
"Wow, you can admit that."

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:10.040
But I was walking around downtown Davis,

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:12.720
I see the poster for "Harold
&amp; Kumar Go To White Castle"

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.920
And I saw John Cho and
Kal Penn on that poster,

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:16.468
and I remember going
into the movie theater

00:11:16.468 --> 00:11:18.862
and people were laughing and it was packed

00:11:18.862 --> 00:11:23.550
and it was this like
seminal college, weed movie,

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:25.130
which I didn't think was possible.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:29.033
And there was two Asian leads,
they weren't stereotypical.

00:11:30.005 --> 00:11:32.971
And the movie became this cult classic.

00:11:32.971 --> 00:11:36.337
And that was one of the
first times where I thought,

00:11:36.337 --> 00:11:37.850
"Oh, this is possible."

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:41.170
Then in 2005, my sophomore year,

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:43.710
Russell Peters had a big video

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:46.680
that started going viral of
him doing stand up comedy.

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:48.650
And I had been open mic-ing
for about a year and a half

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:50.370
at that point, and I couldn't believe it.

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:53.770
He was coming to UCLA to perform

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:56.200
at one of their big auditoriums.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:59.180
And I drove from Sacramento to LA.

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:00.450
I've never told Russell this story,

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:01.450
but I should tell him the story.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:06.210
I paid $88 and I stood way in
the back of this auditorium.

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:08.430
And I watched him and he
was wearing a white blazer,

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:11.330
I'll never forget this,
Chris Spencer opened for him.

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:13.880
Wearing a white blazer,
and I could not believe

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:16.580
there were 1800 people in this theater.

00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:18.960
And he was talking about his family

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:20.040
and talking about his life.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:21.960
And it just, it really clicked for me

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:23.450
in that moment that it was possible

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:26.169
that this kind of weird,
super power that I had,

00:12:26.169 --> 00:12:28.760
it actually was valued by society,

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:30.490
it was seen by society, and you could

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:32.510
actually make a living doing it.

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:36.050
And then the next 15 years of
my life was just figuring out,

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:39.400
am I good enough to actually
make a living doing it?

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:40.730
But I just came to terms with,

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:44.850
I really love the art form
and I loved the craft of it.

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:47.380
- And you were all in,
there weren't moments

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:48.659
where you were like, "Oh,
maybe I'm gonna go back

00:12:48.659 --> 00:12:51.230
"to what the family wants me to do."

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:52.500
- Yeah, there were a few moments.

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:57.370
I remember when my LSAT
score was expiring,

00:12:57.370 --> 00:12:58.610
that was a big deal.

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:00.720
It was five years after
I graduated college,

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.630
and that was a real tough
conversation with my parents

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:05.080
that I wasn't gonna go.

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:07.220
Then they made me go, they made me not go,

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:08.890
but they made me apply,

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:13.057
and I ended up deferring my admission.

00:13:13.057 --> 00:13:14.897
And then when they asked me,

00:13:14.897 --> 00:13:16.690
"Hey, are you gonna come back or not?"

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:18.700
I ended up turning it down.

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:21.640
And so there were these big kind of

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:22.790
speed bumps along the way,

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:25.489
but I kind of disappointed my parents,

00:13:25.489 --> 00:13:27.100
like, through snake venom.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:28.550
It was about an eight year process

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:30.850
for the venom of disappointment to kick in

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:33.593
and for them to give up on
me and to move on to Ayesha.

00:13:34.477 --> 00:13:37.060
(Sal laughing)

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:39.023
- You have kids, right?

00:13:39.023 --> 00:13:40.130
How many kids do you have?

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:43.130
- I have three kids, 11,
nine, and five and a half.

00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:44.420
- Yeah. I mean, (speaking
foreign language)

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:46.210
You're gonna have to give
up on one of them, perhaps.

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:48.500
Like I got two, I mean, who's
gonna be the golden one.

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:50.473
How many franchise picks do you have?

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:53.090
- That's right, that's why you need,

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:54.740
you need to have a little
bit of a portfolio.

00:13:54.740 --> 00:13:57.020
Just so you know, one
to fully disappoint you.

00:13:57.020 --> 00:14:00.131
- Distributing your Roth
IRA, you know what I mean?

00:14:00.131 --> 00:14:02.050
- (laughs) Exactly, exactly.

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:05.370
Well, what was the moment that
you were kind of accepted?

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:07.531
Was it the gig at "The Daily Show"

00:14:07.531 --> 00:14:08.797
that your parents were like,

00:14:08.797 --> 00:14:11.610
"Oh, we knew all along
we had a gifted son."

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:14.530
- Yeah. So, you know, 2014,

00:14:14.530 --> 00:14:17.232
when John hired me at "The Daily Show",

00:14:17.232 --> 00:14:19.720
it was a real seminal
moment for me in terms

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:22.330
of just like respect from the community,

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:25.070
the comedy community, my parents,

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:28.050
because it's very rare
that you get to do things

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:29.890
that are both funny and meaningful.

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:31.690
Like there's a lot of things
that you do in show business

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:35.130
that are just like, just sheer funny.

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:36.650
And then there's things
that are just pure,

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:38.730
like philanthropy, that are meaningful.

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:39.870
And John Stewart kind of

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:42.130
represented the culmination of both.

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:44.700
And the show is an
institution, it's like SNL.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:47.990
So getting hired to be at "The
Daily Show" was this moment

00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:49.797
for, especially my family to be like,

00:14:49.797 --> 00:14:53.920
"Look, I think he should
do this as a career."

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:57.300
At that point, I was
already what, 10 years,

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:00.110
one month, nine days into
doing standup comedy,

00:15:00.110 --> 00:15:01.993
so it was what it was.

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:04.703
- Two questions.

00:15:04.703 --> 00:15:07.973
What do you think John Stewart saw in you?

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:09.993
Was he right?

00:15:09.993 --> 00:15:11.220
- Do you mean that as a hater

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:12.850
or do you mean that genuinely?

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:14.880
Like is a positive way?

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:17.033
- A very positive way if I was a hater,

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:21.382
we wouldn't be talking right now. (laughs)

00:15:21.382 --> 00:15:26.382
- I think he really loves
take and perspective.

00:15:30.374 --> 00:15:33.500
When I knew that Michael
Che had been hired

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:35.850
to join Saturday night live,

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:37.650
there was an opening at the show.

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:40.250
And when I submitted my audition tape,

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:44.760
I just felt like I felt
very uniquely positioned.

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:46.230
I felt like I could be on the show.

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:48.640
I really do feel like
I would be a good fit.

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:50.860
And so I made my own audition tape.

00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:53.470
I made my own field piece,
and I sent it to them.

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:56.970
It was almost like a proof
of concept, and they loved it

00:15:56.970 --> 00:15:59.480
and they had me come in
and do a screen test.

00:15:59.480 --> 00:16:02.130
And I remember for my screen test,

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:03.770
I really wanted to swing for the fences.

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:06.780
And so I took some big
chances and the screen test,

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:08.070
I made fun of John.

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:11.300
I took some liberties even in the script

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:12.710
that I had submitted them,

00:16:12.710 --> 00:16:15.960
and now that I look back on it,

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:17.500
and maybe one day I'll
put it up on YouTube

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:19.400
or something so people can see,

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:23.200
I'm really glad that I stuck to my guns.

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:26.010
I made fun of the boss
in front of everybody,

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:27.650
and I swung for the fences.

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:28.810
I wasn't trying to get a single,

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:31.810
I tried to hit this thing over
the fence and get a home run.

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:37.500
- And once you were on the
show, what did you learn there?

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:38.690
What did you learn from John Stewart

00:16:38.690 --> 00:16:41.008
or the other folks on the show?

00:16:41.008 --> 00:16:44.710
- I realized that take is
more important than jokes.

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:46.670
One of the things I learned early on was

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:49.030
when John would walk into
the 9:15 a.m. meeting,

00:16:49.030 --> 00:16:51.160
he wasn't trying to be funny.

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:53.760
He really was distilling his opinion

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:55.760
into two or three sentences.

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:58.210
And then we would build the
entire show around that,

00:16:58.210 --> 00:17:01.210
and that's the most
important thing for comedy.

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:02.780
You're making a funny argument,

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:04.890
but at the core what is your argument?

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:07.840
And so great jokes at their essence

00:17:07.840 --> 00:17:11.023
are great philosophical
positions said in a funny way.

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:15.410
- And I think that is what
really separated "The Daily Show"

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:16.670
from the rest of the pack.

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:19.190
I mean, in the early 2000s it became,

00:17:19.190 --> 00:17:20.280
a lot of people start to realize

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:21.450
that they were giving in some ways

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:24.490
more truthful information
than a lot of what

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:26.180
you were getting in the rest of the media.

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:28.510
Do you see that as part of, when you look

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:32.210
30, 40, 50 years back on your career,

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:34.237
are you saying, "Hey, I'm gonna be happy

00:17:34.237 --> 00:17:35.740
"if I just entertained a bunch of folks"?

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:39.039
Are you saying, "No, I also
want to change society."

00:17:39.039 --> 00:17:43.219
- Yeah, look, comedy isn't
medicine, medicine is medicine.

00:17:43.219 --> 00:17:44.590
(laughs)

00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:46.987
So you hear a lot of debates about like,

00:17:46.987 --> 00:17:49.457
"Why didn't this song change the world?"

00:17:49.457 --> 00:17:52.937
"Hey, if this person was still on the air,

00:17:52.937 --> 00:17:54.720
"Donald Trump wouldn't be president."

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:56.820
None of those things are true.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:59.250
I think art is a true
reflection of the times.

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:04.250
And my goal, Sal, you get this,
I'll button it with this is,

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:07.570
I use comedy as an art
form to understand myself.

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:10.970
And so the work that I do
is to present my thoughts

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:13.210
and my viewpoint of the world to people

00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:14.280
so that they feel seen,

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:16.090
so that I feel seen,
and I feel understood.

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:18.100
I want to connect with
people and I want to

00:18:18.100 --> 00:18:20.290
explain the human
condition through comedy.

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:22.930
And ultimately I want to say
how I really feel in my heart

00:18:22.930 --> 00:18:24.240
and not be a darpok.

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:26.380
So if 20, 30, 40 years from now,

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:28.610
I look back on my life and I
was like, I wasn't a darpok,

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:30.862
and I really said it, I really said it.

00:18:30.862 --> 00:18:33.650
And you look back on our show,

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:35.450
I'm proud of our Saudi Arabia episode.

00:18:35.450 --> 00:18:37.160
I'm proud of our Indian elections episode.

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:39.770
I'm proud of our student loans episode

00:18:39.770 --> 00:18:41.630
because we swung for it,

00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:45.363
and that's what it's really about for me.

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:48.780
- That's powerful, and just to translate,

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:49.920
do more technical jargon,

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:52.470
and I'm not the most qualified
person to translate Hindi,

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:54.150
but darpok means

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:55.610
kind of scaredy cat.
- Scaredy cat.

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:59.990
- Scaredy cat would be
the (laughs) the best.

00:18:59.990 --> 00:19:01.993
- You look like also one of my ethos is.

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:04.980
Sal, I know you're the Khan Academy guy,

00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:07.090
but I also don't want to explain myself,

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:10.270
like I want to be the most
authentic version of myself.

00:19:10.270 --> 00:19:13.300
And so I want to make
the world a bigger place

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:15.680
instead of trying to bend
and try to communicate

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:19.020
and explain my culture
point of view to people.

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:20.910
I want to bring you into my world

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:22.140
and make people feel like that.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:24.390
And so I think that's when
the show's at its best.

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:27.090
And honestly, audiences
are really smart and savvy.

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:29.623
They want to have an
authentic, real experience.

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:33.500
- And I'll say, I'll just speak
for myself very personally.

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:35.323
I think I grew up, I'm a
little bit older than you.

00:19:35.323 --> 00:19:36.790
I think I'm nine or 10
years older than you,

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:39.651
but in my generation, so to speak,

00:19:39.651 --> 00:19:43.331
it was kind of like, "Well,
I'll just keep that to myself.

00:19:43.331 --> 00:19:45.772
"I'll just try my best to fit in."

00:19:45.772 --> 00:19:50.170
When I saw your Netflix
special "Homecoming King",

00:19:50.170 --> 00:19:52.140
and obviously a lot of your
episodes of "Patriot Act",

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:54.930
there is a little bit of
me that kinda got unlocked.

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:58.447
I'm like, "Yeah, yeah,
that's right." (laughs)

00:19:58.447 --> 00:20:00.825
I shouldn't be hiding that as much.

00:20:00.825 --> 00:20:03.270
- The game used to be, and your generation

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:06.660
really fought for this where
assimilation was the win.

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:09.507
My generation really I'm trying to say,

00:20:09.507 --> 00:20:11.540
"Authenticity is the win,"

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:14.730
and I think hopefully for
my kids and the younger,

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:17.410
I see these young comics coming up,

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:19.240
they don't even need
to introduce themselves

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:22.020
when they walk into the
room and I love that.

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:24.972
They don't even need to
provide the backstory.

00:20:24.972 --> 00:20:27.070
I feel like a lot of what the generation

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:29.350
before me was providing was the prequel.

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:30.380
They had to tell the story of our parents,

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:32.890
they had to tell the
story of our community.

00:20:32.890 --> 00:20:37.500
And I think now there's a
whole bunch of like young kids

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:40.860
that are out there making
really cool work on the internet

00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:43.540
where they don't have to
introduce themselves to the world.

00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:45.677
They don't have to carry that baggage.

00:20:45.677 --> 00:20:47.850
I think the art that they're going to make

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:50.730
is gonna be even that
much more interesting.

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:52.830
- Yeah, and there's a lot
of questions coming in.

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:57.087
This is from Instagram,
this is Amoogi says,

00:20:57.087 --> 00:20:58.247
"As a fellow brown, Indian,

00:20:58.247 --> 00:21:00.890
"how do you deal with
racism during school?"

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:02.680
And I'll ask that in a broader context,

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:05.310
obviously race is a very big
topic right now in our society.

00:21:05.310 --> 00:21:08.130
And you've spoken about it in, I think,

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:10.130
very interesting ways, especially 'cause

00:21:11.560 --> 00:21:14.290
a lot of the dialogue is
around black versus white.

00:21:14.290 --> 00:21:15.450
Sometimes people will say brown,

00:21:15.450 --> 00:21:18.150
but brown may or may not
apply to South Asians or not.

00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:20.583
How have you thought about it?

00:21:21.743 --> 00:21:25.150
In a lot of your shows you've definitely

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:27.481
brought up race as a major-

00:21:27.481 --> 00:21:30.690
- Yeah, we did a big
episode about George Floyd

00:21:30.690 --> 00:21:32.250
and the murder of George Floyd,

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:35.110
and really like the Asian
Americans community,

00:21:35.110 --> 00:21:37.350
our community's
involvement in all of that.

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:39.830
And for the longest
time, like you said Sal,

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:42.680
race has been seen as a black
white issue in this country.

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:43.587
And our community has been like,

00:21:43.587 --> 00:21:46.030
"Where's our place in all of that?"

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:48.570
And one of the things that
I wanted to explain was,

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:49.940
for a lot of us were descendants

00:21:49.940 --> 00:21:51.620
of the 1965 Immigration Act.

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:53.770
So because of that piece of legislation

00:21:53.770 --> 00:21:56.900
signed by Lyndon B. Johnson
and Martin Luther King, Jr.,

00:21:56.900 --> 00:21:59.110
Our ammi and our aabu's
were able to come here

00:21:59.110 --> 00:22:03.840
in the 70s and 80s to get
educational opportunities.

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:06.630
So we could be here and
live in Silicon Valley

00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:09.223
and live in Fremont and
Milpitas, et cetera, right?

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:14.070
That was important for
me to connect the dots

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:16.553
and say, "No, we're integrally linked

00:22:17.715 --> 00:22:19.997
"to the civil rights
movement in this country,

00:22:19.997 --> 00:22:21.870
"and we were CC'd on that email."

00:22:21.870 --> 00:22:24.680
So it's our job to be actively
involved in the social change

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:26.330
that we want to see in the world.

00:22:27.770 --> 00:22:30.080
- Yeah, what I often
point to family members,

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:32.910
and you've often talked
about even the racism

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:34.520
that exists within our community,

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:36.770
which is sometimes quite strong.

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:39.670
But what I always like to
remind folks in our community

00:22:39.670 --> 00:22:42.300
is that our lives would be very different

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:43.800
if not for the civil rights movement.

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:45.987
I think a lot of South Asians kind of say,

00:22:45.987 --> 00:22:47.587
"Yeah, we've assimilated.

00:22:47.587 --> 00:22:50.077
"We've got these houses in the suburbs.

00:22:50.077 --> 00:22:52.498
"We're were doctors and
lawyers and engineers."

00:22:52.498 --> 00:22:54.890
But if you did not have the very hard work

00:22:54.890 --> 00:22:56.330
of the civil rights movement, one,

00:22:56.330 --> 00:22:57.630
the immigration act wouldn't have passed,

00:22:57.630 --> 00:23:00.340
but even if our parents
were able to immigrate

00:23:00.340 --> 00:23:02.530
to this country, their lives
would have been very different

00:23:02.530 --> 00:23:04.340
if not for desegregation
and things like that.

00:23:04.340 --> 00:23:05.984
- Yeah, and look, I'm not trying to be,

00:23:05.984 --> 00:23:08.070
I think people have these very

00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:09.950
intellectual arguments about it

00:23:09.950 --> 00:23:12.970
where they go, "Yeah, but look
at what I did in my life."

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:14.340
Really what, a lot of the stuff,

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:16.454
the conversation that's
happening right now

00:23:16.454 --> 00:23:19.203
about different communities and people,

00:23:20.870 --> 00:23:24.073
folks want to be heard,
it's an empathy argument.

00:23:24.920 --> 00:23:26.440
People are really hurting right now, man.

00:23:26.440 --> 00:23:28.680
Like unemployment is at an all time high,

00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:31.920
people are really suffering
and what's happening right now,

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:33.730
both the social and the, hopefully,

00:23:33.730 --> 00:23:36.290
I think the economic reckoning
that's gonna be happening

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:38.050
is people want to be heard.

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:41.640
And I think we have to be empathetic

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:44.673
to hear the life
experiences of other people.

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:47.870
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:23:47.870 --> 00:23:49.120
Well, I want to make sure I get enough,

00:23:49.120 --> 00:23:51.810
there's so many questions
coming in from YouTube.

00:23:51.810 --> 00:23:54.520
So this is from Lizzy Ammabude,

00:23:54.520 --> 00:23:56.080
and you know, we talked
a little bit about it.

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:56.913
It starts off with

00:23:56.913 --> 00:23:58.280
"Mr. Minhaj, did your
parents support you?"

00:23:58.280 --> 00:23:59.113
We talked a bit about that,

00:23:59.113 --> 00:24:01.517
but if not, "How did you
get them to deal with it?

00:24:01.517 --> 00:24:03.667
"'Cause I'm about to
start studying medicine,

00:24:03.667 --> 00:24:06.437
"and I really feel that it's
not where I'm meant to be,

00:24:06.437 --> 00:24:08.407
"but my parents," I
think it didn't complete,

00:24:08.407 --> 00:24:09.470
"but my parents want me to,"

00:24:09.470 --> 00:24:11.270
I'm assuming is what Lizzy's saying.

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:17.270
- I think those tough conversations
are really hard to have,

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:20.520
but I've told this to a lot
of kids and a lot of people

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:22.220
that have turned to me for advice.

00:24:23.720 --> 00:24:25.220
When you transition into adulthood,

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:27.810
there's probably going
to be two major decisions

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:29.170
that you have to make.

00:24:29.170 --> 00:24:31.320
And they may be really, really tough

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:32.920
to talk about with your parents,

00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:35.950
who you choose to love and what you choose

00:24:35.950 --> 00:24:37.750
to do with your profession.

00:24:37.750 --> 00:24:40.202
Those two hills are worth dying on.

00:24:40.202 --> 00:24:44.810
I'll say that unequivocally,
everything else,

00:24:44.810 --> 00:24:46.650
everything else just
side with your parents.

00:24:46.650 --> 00:24:48.680
They want you to come
there for Thanksgiving,

00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:50.450
your hala wants you to do this,

00:24:50.450 --> 00:24:52.710
your chachi wants you to do that, do it.

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:54.776
Just be like fine, I'll do it.

00:24:54.776 --> 00:24:57.273
Those two fights are worth having.

00:24:58.670 --> 00:24:59.503
- That's super powerful,

00:24:59.503 --> 00:25:02.950
and what would you say to the,
'cause I know people even,

00:25:02.950 --> 00:25:07.020
I've faced it myself,
starting a not-for-profit

00:25:07.020 --> 00:25:09.483
caused significant cognitive dissonance.

00:25:10.440 --> 00:25:13.040
Doing stuff on YouTube
2005 created significant

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:14.570
cognitive dissonance with my family.

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:16.567
And they're like, "Well it seems academic

00:25:16.567 --> 00:25:19.010
"and related to computers,
so that's good.'

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:21.470
But it literally says not profit. (laughs)

00:25:21.470 --> 00:25:23.658
It's on you.
- Wait, wait,

00:25:23.658 --> 00:25:25.430
there's no money?

00:25:25.430 --> 00:25:27.127
Sal, there's no money.

00:25:27.127 --> 00:25:29.140
- There's no money.

00:25:29.140 --> 00:25:30.323
- No prestige.
- I remember.

00:25:30.323 --> 00:25:33.210
- How can we flex on other
people in the community?

00:25:33.210 --> 00:25:35.660
This is really bad, why
can't you be like Farrah?

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:38.700
- No, I would like to
claim that I don't have

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:41.610
a fragile male ego, but I
remember going to dinner parties

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:43.850
in 2005, 2000, well actually 2009

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:46.190
when I quit my very acceptable job

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:48.740
as an investment analyst at a hedge fund.

00:25:48.740 --> 00:25:50.139
And I go to dinner parties,

00:25:50.139 --> 00:25:53.890
primarily South Asian dinner
parties, and I've heard people,

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:56.190
I literally had this one moment where

00:25:56.190 --> 00:25:58.460
after I explained what I did for a living,

00:25:58.460 --> 00:26:01.350
but I was living off
of savings at the time,

00:26:01.350 --> 00:26:04.317
the couple I heard them in earshot saying,

00:26:04.317 --> 00:26:06.760
"Thank God, his wife is
training to be a physician."

00:26:06.760 --> 00:26:09.410
And you know, that was
definitely a punch in the gut.

00:26:10.660 --> 00:26:13.940
- Yeah, but I think if, look, if COVID

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:16.040
has exposed anything, now
that we're all just like,

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:19.563
holed up at home, who are
we stunting for anyways?

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:24.600
If you make major life
decisions that are gonna

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:26.760
impact your life for
the next 10 to 15 years,

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:30.030
like med school, which is
a 10 to 12 year proposition

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:31.766
plus all the time that you have to work

00:26:31.766 --> 00:26:35.650
and the debt you have just to stunt

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:38.120
on some random auntie in Natomas,

00:26:38.120 --> 00:26:40.000
good God man, that ain't worth it.

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:40.980
Like, it just ain't worth it.

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:43.140
And I remember like my freshman
year when I was premed,

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:46.400
I thought it would be
worth it, but it's not.

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:48.700
And so I'm not saying
that it doesn't hurt.

00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:51.900
Like, being made fun of in
the Sacramento community,

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:52.733
that's not fun.

00:26:52.733 --> 00:26:55.130
Like being told the decisions
you're making are wrong

00:26:55.130 --> 00:26:58.347
or being told, like
hearing people whisper,

00:26:58.347 --> 00:27:01.810
"I pray that you don't end up like that."

00:27:01.810 --> 00:27:05.717
That's hurtful, but I don't know,

00:27:07.250 --> 00:27:10.240
like also waking up and loving what you do

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:11.833
and feeling purpose in it,

00:27:13.010 --> 00:27:15.723
I don't know, it means
more to me than that.

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:17.780
- Yeah, super powerful.

00:27:17.780 --> 00:27:18.770
You've got one life.

00:27:18.770 --> 00:27:20.220
I mean, the time has gone by.

00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:21.893
We could talk for hours,

00:27:21.893 --> 00:27:23.790
but I'll just let you,
on kind of a final word.

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:26.796
There's a lot of questions
about breaking into comedy,

00:27:26.796 --> 00:27:31.580
your inspiration, finish
it however you like,

00:27:31.580 --> 00:27:34.790
but this general thing
of life advice for kids,

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:36.280
how do you pursue your passion?

00:27:36.280 --> 00:27:37.890
I mean, you've already
touched on it a bit.

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:39.860
How do you keep that sticktuitiveness

00:27:41.330 --> 00:27:42.830
to get to where you want to go in life

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:44.360
and have real purpose?

00:27:44.360 --> 00:27:49.320
- Yeah, I think, I think a
really interesting thing.

00:27:49.320 --> 00:27:51.250
I got two kids, I have a two year old,

00:27:51.250 --> 00:27:56.250
and I have a newborn
who's five months old now,

00:27:56.670 --> 00:28:00.500
and the biggest thing
that I wanted to change

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:02.870
from the time I was a
kid to now I'm a father,

00:28:02.870 --> 00:28:05.210
like the Simba/Mufasa relationship,

00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:08.920
is for our parents, they
saw us children as property.

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:11.980
Like you are my investment
and I need to move you

00:28:13.050 --> 00:28:15.520
within the S&amp;P 500, the way you need

00:28:15.520 --> 00:28:17.220
to return on an investment for me,

00:28:18.200 --> 00:28:20.070
I want to look at them less as an owner.

00:28:20.070 --> 00:28:22.280
I want to be more as
somebody who guides them

00:28:22.280 --> 00:28:25.740
and pulls their talents and
their gifts out of them.

00:28:25.740 --> 00:28:30.090
And it's important, if I
was giving advice to again,

00:28:30.090 --> 00:28:34.210
16, 17, 18 year old Hasan,
I would basically say,

00:28:34.210 --> 00:28:36.320
I wouldn't ask him,
"What do you want to do?"

00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:37.817
Because people ask that all the time,

00:28:37.817 --> 00:28:40.150
"Hey, what do you want to
do, what are your dreams?"

00:28:40.150 --> 00:28:42.130
I would say, write down
what are your gifts.

00:28:42.130 --> 00:28:43.865
Meaning, what are the things that you do,

00:28:43.865 --> 00:28:45.850
or even if you rolled out of bed,

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:48.320
you would be better than
everybody else in your class.

00:28:48.320 --> 00:28:49.730
That's your gift.

00:28:49.730 --> 00:28:51.594
And it can be something stupid.

00:28:51.594 --> 00:28:53.110
It can be video games.

00:28:53.110 --> 00:28:54.990
It could be like two hand touch.

00:28:54.990 --> 00:28:56.710
It could be running track.

00:28:56.710 --> 00:28:57.960
It could be any of those things.

00:28:57.960 --> 00:29:00.510
But what is that natural alacrity

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:03.130
that you have where you're
better than everybody

00:29:03.130 --> 00:29:04.970
in your class in your community.

00:29:04.970 --> 00:29:07.270
You can just do it in your sleep.

00:29:07.270 --> 00:29:10.217
I used to have a friend, we
would play pickup basketball.

00:29:10.217 --> 00:29:14.040
He could dunk two handed
in Timberland boots

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:16.060
while he was drinking
Mountain Dew Code Red.

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:17.990
He had a gift for jumping.
(Sal laughing)

00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:18.823
You know what I mean?

00:29:18.823 --> 00:29:21.030
Like if I had a Whopper
and Mountain Dew Code Red,

00:29:21.030 --> 00:29:23.060
there's no way I'm running line drills.

00:29:23.060 --> 00:29:26.890
But my buddy Parnell could
just two hand dunk like that

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:31.200
in boots and cargos, and he
had a gift for that thing.

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:33.670
And so I think the key is figuring out

00:29:33.670 --> 00:29:37.930
what your gifts are and then
applying work ethic to that.

00:29:37.930 --> 00:29:39.370
I wasn't gifted at math.

00:29:39.370 --> 00:29:42.200
I wasn't gifted at certain
subjects in school.

00:29:42.200 --> 00:29:43.033
That wasn't my gift.

00:29:43.033 --> 00:29:44.410
I could work hard at
it, but there's a cap.

00:29:44.410 --> 00:29:46.520
And so try to find what your gift is,

00:29:46.520 --> 00:29:48.863
and don't be ashamed
if it's a weird thing.

00:29:50.430 --> 00:29:51.880
- That's super powerful,
and I think there's

00:29:51.880 --> 00:29:53.330
another layer to that which is,

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:55.080
I think there's more
outlets for more gifts

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:57.161
than maybe at any other time in history.

00:29:57.161 --> 00:30:00.860
As your story highlights,
in some ways mine,

00:30:00.860 --> 00:30:02.520
and many, many other folks right now.

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:04.385
Thank you so much.
- Oh yeah man.

00:30:04.385 --> 00:30:05.590
You know what the encouraging thing is?

00:30:05.590 --> 00:30:07.010
It's just the beginning.

00:30:07.010 --> 00:30:09.420
Like there are people that are on YouTube

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:11.117
that do unboxing videos that are

00:30:11.117 --> 00:30:15.350
just savants of their craft, right?

00:30:15.350 --> 00:30:17.360
There's people that play video games.

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:21.593
There's so many different ways
to be unique or interesting.

00:30:22.500 --> 00:30:24.150
I have a friend who has a great voice

00:30:24.150 --> 00:30:26.750
for podcasting, and he
has an incredible podcast.

00:30:26.750 --> 00:30:30.070
Like those are things that just
didn't exist five years ago.

00:30:30.070 --> 00:30:32.770
And so try to look at what your gifts are,

00:30:32.770 --> 00:30:34.600
stop trying to be somebody else

00:30:34.600 --> 00:30:35.720
and figure out what those gifts are.

00:30:35.720 --> 00:30:38.340
And I think if there ever was
a time to really understand

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:40.951
what those things are, now's the time.

00:30:40.951 --> 00:30:42.730
- Yeah, that's super powerful.

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:45.360
And I think, very, very wise words

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:47.660
for everyone listening at all ages.

00:30:47.660 --> 00:30:49.510
Hasan, thanks so much for joining.

00:30:49.510 --> 00:30:51.740
This was a real treat, I
hope we can do this again.

00:30:51.740 --> 00:30:52.573
- Thanks Sal.

00:30:55.300 --> 00:30:58.150
- Well, thanks everyone
for joining another,

00:30:58.150 --> 00:31:00.070
I thought really, I never know where

00:31:00.070 --> 00:31:01.370
these conversations are gonna go,

00:31:01.370 --> 00:31:03.230
but I thought there
were some definite gems

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:06.210
of wisdom from Hasan just now
on just how to live your life.

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:08.600
How do you live your life with purpose,

00:31:08.600 --> 00:31:12.810
and how do you stick
to what you believe in

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:14.290
and your gifts in the world?

00:31:14.290 --> 00:31:15.840
So I thought that was a very powerful.

00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:17.767
I'm gonna make sure my kids hear that,

00:31:17.767 --> 00:31:19.973
and I'm going to make
sure that I, as a parent,

00:31:19.973 --> 00:31:21.610
don't fall into some of the traps

00:31:21.610 --> 00:31:25.593
that at least both Has
and I were talking about

00:31:25.593 --> 00:31:27.160
that you sometimes see.

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:29.180
I'm super excited about tomorrow's guest,

00:31:29.180 --> 00:31:32.180
Nikole Hannah-Jones, a
journalist and author

00:31:32.180 --> 00:31:35.279
of the 1619 Project.

00:31:35.279 --> 00:31:38.745
1619 is the year that
slavery came to the Americas,

00:31:38.745 --> 00:31:41.160
so obviously a very relevant topic

00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:45.241
to the world, what's going
on in the world as we speak.

00:31:45.241 --> 00:31:49.010
But regardless, super excited
to have all of y'all join us,

00:31:49.010 --> 00:31:51.023
and I will see you tomorrow.

00:31:52.780 --> 00:31:55.030
(chiming)

